WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:01.560
This is transduction.

00:01.560 --> 00:04.320
So basically, how to get into the blood-brain barrier.

00:04.320 --> 00:06.080
Thank you, Alex.

00:06.080 --> 00:07.560
Thank you so much for that warm welcome,

00:07.560 --> 00:08.680
and good morning, everyone.

00:08.680 --> 00:10.600
My name is Xin-Hong Alex Chung,

00:10.600 --> 00:12.280
and I'm from the Grotto Narrow Lab,

00:12.280 --> 00:13.800
and I'm excited to share with you

00:13.800 --> 00:17.440
my talk titled CUNY-AEV Interactions with AVR

00:17.440 --> 00:20.520
and LRP6 Toggle Specters Between Vascular

00:20.520 --> 00:22.200
and Neuron Selective Tropism.

00:23.760 --> 00:25.520
A lot of the recent efforts in the field

00:25.520 --> 00:27.760
have focused on identifying receptors

00:27.840 --> 00:30.200
that are being mediated by AVs

00:30.200 --> 00:33.200
for effective transportation across the BBB.

00:33.200 --> 00:36.640
There are receptors such as L6A for PHPB,

00:36.640 --> 00:41.640
LRP6 for AV9X1, and Car4 for 9P31, amongst others.

00:44.520 --> 00:46.920
And even more recently, a lot of focus

00:46.920 --> 00:49.800
has been garnered towards targeting these receptors

00:49.800 --> 00:51.680
for more effective engineering

00:51.680 --> 00:53.560
so that we have increased predictability

00:53.560 --> 00:56.800
when we're thinking about future therapeutics.

00:56.800 --> 00:58.520
As we're going into this landscape

00:58.520 --> 01:00.320
of thinking about future therapeutics,

01:00.320 --> 01:01.720
I believe that there is something

01:01.720 --> 01:03.360
that we should also consider,

01:03.360 --> 01:05.920
which is how binding affinity to these receptors

01:05.920 --> 01:07.280
might play an important factor

01:07.280 --> 01:10.200
when we're thinking about predicting tropism.

01:10.200 --> 01:11.960
Work that's done in the antibody

01:11.960 --> 01:13.840
and nanobody engineering field

01:13.840 --> 01:16.560
has shown that maybe there could be a Goldilocks zone

01:16.560 --> 01:18.360
for binding affinity to a receptor

01:18.360 --> 01:21.080
for effective transactosis across the BBB.

01:22.400 --> 01:24.960
In work done by Bien Nai et al,

01:24.960 --> 01:27.520
they show that some engineered antibodies

01:27.520 --> 01:29.160
that target transferrin receptors

01:29.160 --> 01:31.360
with too high of a binding affinity

01:31.360 --> 01:33.920
ends up getting stuck in these endothelial cells

01:33.920 --> 01:37.280
and don't actually make it across effectively through the BBB.

01:37.280 --> 01:40.480
Conversely, those binders that are too weak

01:40.480 --> 01:42.360
to bind to transferrin receptor

01:42.360 --> 01:44.760
actually never made it through the BBB

01:44.760 --> 01:47.200
because perhaps they were being outcompeted

01:47.200 --> 01:50.280
by endogenous ligands against the transferrin receptor.

01:50.280 --> 01:54.240
And thus, they found that this medium zone

01:54.240 --> 01:57.560
of moderate binding to the transferrin receptor

01:57.560 --> 01:59.520
was the optimal binding affinity

01:59.520 --> 02:02.920
to help for effective transactosis across the BBB.

02:02.920 --> 02:06.040
And we wondered if the same principle could apply to AAVs

02:06.040 --> 02:09.080
as it's trying to cross the blood-brain barrier as well.

02:09.080 --> 02:11.080
And for this, I'm focusing on predominantly

02:11.080 --> 02:14.000
two receptors, AAVR and LRP6.

02:16.840 --> 02:19.120
And we're also looking at our engineered vector,

02:19.120 --> 02:22.080
AAV9X1, and how we can start engineering

02:22.080 --> 02:24.000
future variants of this vector

02:24.000 --> 02:26.720
in hopes that it can become more neurotropic.

02:26.720 --> 02:30.200
AAV9X1 was a previously engineered vector from our lab

02:30.200 --> 02:34.160
which targets AAVR and LRP6 for effective transduction

02:34.160 --> 02:38.720
of endothelial cells, specifically in the CNS of mouse

02:38.720 --> 02:40.360
when they're delivered systemically.

02:41.400 --> 02:44.480
And we wondered if this targeting of endothelial cells

02:44.480 --> 02:49.480
is because of the fact that AAV9X1 is binding too tightly

02:49.800 --> 02:52.800
to AAVR and LRP6 and ended up getting stuck

02:52.800 --> 02:54.280
in these endothelial cells.

02:54.280 --> 02:57.400
And therefore, by weakening its interaction to AAVR

02:57.400 --> 03:00.080
and LRP6, could we see more effective transduction

03:06.080 --> 03:07.880
of the cytosis across the BBB?

03:10.260 --> 03:12.760
So first, I wanted to go and independently

03:12.760 --> 03:14.680
weaken its interaction with AAVR

03:14.680 --> 03:17.080
in hopes to create a more neurotropic vector.

03:17.080 --> 03:20.280
And this stem from recent work that was published in our lab

03:20.280 --> 03:22.080
would showcase that KAPI-10,

03:22.080 --> 03:24.760
which is a further evolved vector from HPEB,

03:24.760 --> 03:26.760
which modified the VR4 loop

03:26.760 --> 03:29.160
to have further detargeting from PKD2,

03:29.160 --> 03:31.240
the binding domain of AAVR.

03:31.240 --> 03:33.680
This change actually was the reason why

03:33.680 --> 03:36.720
there was increased specificity in targeting neurons

03:36.720 --> 03:40.560
in the CNS of mice when this vector, KAPI-10,

03:40.560 --> 03:42.640
was delivered systemically.

03:42.640 --> 03:45.500
And we wondered if by introducing this KAPI-10 loop

03:45.500 --> 03:49.640
into AAV9X1, we could also have this drastic decrease

03:49.640 --> 03:51.640
in its binding affinity to AAVR.

03:51.640 --> 03:53.680
Unfortunately, by just simply substituting

03:53.680 --> 03:57.480
the same VR4 loop onto AAV9X1,

03:57.480 --> 04:01.280
did not dramatically decrease its binding affinity to AAVR.

04:01.280 --> 04:03.240
And therefore, we need to come up with novel ways

04:03.240 --> 04:05.600
to detarget from AAVR.

04:05.600 --> 04:07.360
And luckily, we were able to recently

04:07.360 --> 04:10.140
solve the cryo-EM structure of its interaction,

04:10.140 --> 04:13.860
AAV9X1's interaction with AAVR.

04:13.860 --> 04:16.520
And we were able to identify key residues

04:16.520 --> 04:19.680
that might be responsible for its binding to AAVR.

04:19.680 --> 04:24.080
And by targeting these locuses for effective rational

04:24.080 --> 04:27.480
engineering, we were able to create this beta mutation.

04:27.480 --> 04:30.680
And by introducing this beta mutation to AAV9X1,

04:30.680 --> 04:32.640
we were able to see a dramatic decrease

04:32.640 --> 04:35.600
in its binding affinity to AAVR.

04:35.600 --> 04:38.360
And when we delivered this vector, AAV9X1 beta,

04:38.360 --> 04:40.720
that we're calling, into mice systemically,

04:40.720 --> 04:42.960
we were able to see that this vector became

04:42.960 --> 04:46.480
a pretty specific vector in targeting neurons.

04:46.480 --> 04:49.360
And I think many of you guys might be thinking that AAV9X1

04:49.360 --> 04:52.560
beta is a pretty bad vector when it's targeting neurons

04:52.560 --> 04:55.360
altogether, because it's not targeting that many cells.

04:55.360 --> 04:58.760
And I thought the same thing when I saw these vectors.

04:58.760 --> 05:02.040
And so I wanted to play around with the second receptor, LRP6.

05:02.040 --> 05:04.840
And to see if, by weakening LRP6's interaction

05:04.840 --> 05:08.480
with AAV9X1, we could have a more increased potency

05:08.480 --> 05:10.880
when it comes to targeting neurons.

05:10.880 --> 05:14.200
However, unlike AAVR and AAV9X1,

05:14.200 --> 05:16.120
we don't have a solved cryoenium structure

05:16.120 --> 05:18.840
to identify the specific loci.

05:18.840 --> 05:20.720
And for that, we actually ended up

05:20.720 --> 05:23.920
using a more traditional method by having a scanning tumor

05:23.920 --> 05:27.960
library across a VR8 loop of AAV9X1

05:27.960 --> 05:30.480
to create mutants that could have various binding affinities

05:30.480 --> 05:31.920
to LRP6.

05:31.920 --> 05:36.440
We subjected this library into a magnetic bead conjugated E1,

05:36.440 --> 05:39.440
E2 subdomains of LRP6, and pulled them down.

05:39.440 --> 05:43.120
And we were able to analyze the enrichment values

05:43.120 --> 05:47.560
of the capsits that bound most tightly to LRP6

05:47.560 --> 05:49.240
and those that did not.

05:49.240 --> 05:52.560
And as expected, we were able to see that AAV9X1 is

05:52.560 --> 05:54.360
near the top of the ranking profile,

05:54.360 --> 05:57.840
because we know that AAV9X1 binds with LRP6.

05:57.840 --> 06:00.480
And conversely, AAV9 tends to be at the bottom

06:00.480 --> 06:01.920
of this ranking profile.

06:01.920 --> 06:03.920
And so you guys can think of this ranking profile

06:03.920 --> 06:07.440
as a proxy for binding affinities towards LRP6.

06:07.440 --> 06:09.160
And when we delivered some of the vectors

06:09.160 --> 06:11.720
that were more similar in ranking to AAV9X1,

06:11.720 --> 06:16.240
such as UX5, into my systemically,

06:16.240 --> 06:18.440
we were able to see that its transaction profile is

06:18.440 --> 06:20.520
predominantly still endothelial.

06:20.520 --> 06:22.520
Conversely, variants such as UX1,

06:22.520 --> 06:24.680
which are similar in ranking to AAV9,

06:24.680 --> 06:27.400
and very little transaction altogether.

06:27.400 --> 06:30.920
And when we looked at vectors with higher ranking than UX5

06:30.920 --> 06:34.040
or AAV9X1, we saw that it is still pretty endothelial

06:34.040 --> 06:35.560
in its targeting.

06:35.560 --> 06:37.360
However, when we started to look at variants

06:37.360 --> 06:40.440
that were in between this AAV9X1's binding affinity

06:40.440 --> 06:42.680
and AAV9, we were able to see vectors

06:42.680 --> 06:45.640
that are starting to transcytose across the BBB

06:45.640 --> 06:48.440
and start targeting some of these aster sites.

06:48.440 --> 06:50.880
And I also thought that this vector,

06:50.880 --> 06:52.600
as many of you guys are thinking right now,

06:52.600 --> 06:53.840
is a pretty messy vector.

06:53.840 --> 06:55.680
It is permeating through the BBB,

06:55.680 --> 06:58.000
but it's not at all specific in terms of the tropism

06:58.000 --> 06:59.760
that we would want to see.

06:59.760 --> 07:01.880
And so we wondered by combining these insights

07:01.880 --> 07:04.320
that we gained from understanding the interaction

07:04.320 --> 07:06.560
with AAVR and LRP6,

07:06.560 --> 07:10.120
we could see a highly potent neurotropic vector.

07:10.120 --> 07:12.960
And just to summarize, starting from AAV9X1,

07:12.960 --> 07:15.800
when we weakened independently just AAVR

07:15.800 --> 07:17.640
by introducing this beta mutation,

07:17.640 --> 07:20.040
we saw a sparsely neuronal vector.

07:21.480 --> 07:23.320
And then when we created this vector

07:23.320 --> 07:25.280
with weakened interaction with LRP6,

07:25.280 --> 07:27.280
but not per se with AAVR,

07:27.280 --> 07:29.440
we were able to get this more permeable vector

07:29.440 --> 07:31.160
that goes across the BBB.

07:32.160 --> 07:34.920
And so we wondered by introducing the same B10 mutation

07:34.920 --> 07:37.360
that we introduced to AAV9X1,

07:37.360 --> 07:39.480
could we see a strongly neuronal vector?

07:40.400 --> 07:44.120
And when we did introduce this beta mutation to UX13,

07:44.120 --> 07:46.880
we were able to see this increased potency

07:46.880 --> 07:48.040
when targeting neurons,

07:48.040 --> 07:51.240
and it is predominantly transusing only neurons

07:51.240 --> 07:52.640
when quantified.

07:55.080 --> 07:57.400
Overall, just to summarize my talk,

07:57.400 --> 08:00.040
I was able to start playing around independently

08:00.040 --> 08:02.880
these two dials, AAVR binding affinity

08:02.880 --> 08:04.760
and LRP6 binding affinity

08:04.760 --> 08:08.040
to get to looking at how these binding affinities

08:08.040 --> 08:12.040
are determining its tropism altogether.

08:12.040 --> 08:14.960
And I hope we can take this into consideration

08:14.960 --> 08:17.400
as we're going into future therapeutics research

08:17.400 --> 08:19.360
and trying to have more predictable outcomes

08:19.360 --> 08:20.780
with our engineering efforts.

08:20.780 --> 08:21.620
Thank you so much,

08:21.620 --> 08:23.680
and I open the floor up for questions.

08:23.680 --> 08:24.520
Thank you.

08:24.520 --> 08:25.360
Thank you.

08:30.720 --> 08:32.240
Questions from the audience?

08:33.880 --> 08:36.220
Hi.

08:36.220 --> 08:41.220
Did you look at vector genomes in peripheral organs,

08:42.160 --> 08:44.200
especially at the early time points?

08:45.120 --> 08:46.480
We analyzed these animals

08:46.480 --> 08:48.840
after three to four weeks post-delivery,

08:48.840 --> 08:51.280
so we didn't really look at really early time points

08:51.280 --> 08:52.560
after delivery.

08:52.560 --> 08:54.600
But an important point that you bring up

08:54.600 --> 08:56.840
is the peripheral organs,

08:56.840 --> 08:58.080
something unique that we saw

08:58.080 --> 09:00.640
with introducing this beta mutation

09:00.640 --> 09:02.960
into these AAV variants,

09:02.960 --> 09:04.200
was that we were starting to see

09:04.200 --> 09:06.640
increased transduction of the peripheral nervous system

09:06.640 --> 09:07.480
as well.

09:07.480 --> 09:09.920
You guys can see pretty much in the spring sum

09:09.920 --> 09:13.040
as one representation of how it would perform

09:13.040 --> 09:14.360
like the other peripheral vectors

09:14.360 --> 09:15.680
that were produced from our lab.

09:15.680 --> 09:17.880
And when we looked at organs such as the distal colon,

09:17.880 --> 09:20.600
we saw increased transduction profile there as well.

09:20.600 --> 09:21.480
Okay.

09:21.480 --> 09:23.600
That's true both on the transduction level

09:23.600 --> 09:27.720
as well as on the vector genome per dipole genome.

09:27.720 --> 09:29.460
We didn't do analysis

09:29.460 --> 09:32.260
about quantifying the number of genomes that were found.

09:32.260 --> 09:35.060
We currently are at the stage of just looking

09:35.060 --> 09:37.260
at the protein level of expression.

09:37.260 --> 09:38.180
Okay, thanks.

09:41.900 --> 09:43.540
Thank you, very interesting talk.

09:44.540 --> 09:46.260
I would like to know what your thoughts

09:46.260 --> 09:50.460
are on the actual role of AVR in like,

09:50.460 --> 09:53.180
why do you see this difference in cell targeting?

09:53.180 --> 09:56.340
Given that AVR should be expressed in every cell,

09:56.340 --> 09:58.820
and there's even some indication

09:58.820 --> 10:00.660
that it might be more a sorting signal

10:00.660 --> 10:03.420
than the actual entry receptor?

10:03.420 --> 10:06.940
I think that's a really important question to consider.

10:06.940 --> 10:08.540
Frankly, I don't quite understand

10:08.540 --> 10:09.860
what's happening here either,

10:09.860 --> 10:11.980
but I know there are a lot of really good talks

10:11.980 --> 10:15.260
that are happening around the role of AVR in transduction.

10:15.260 --> 10:18.060
I think AVR in general is very critical

10:18.060 --> 10:20.020
for transduction altogether.

10:20.020 --> 10:22.460
As many of you guys know, knockouts of AVR

10:22.460 --> 10:24.500
might as completely diminishes

10:25.340 --> 10:27.780
systemic delivered AV transduction.

10:27.780 --> 10:31.500
And I think these vectors are so definitely binding to AVR,

10:31.500 --> 10:34.020
but having this Goldilocks zone of binding

10:34.020 --> 10:37.460
maybe perhaps allows AVR to play more of a trafficking role

10:37.460 --> 10:39.740
rather than more of a sticky web

10:39.740 --> 10:42.020
for targeting off-target regions

10:42.020 --> 10:45.060
of other organs that we don't care about.

10:45.060 --> 10:48.380
Right, so you think you still transduce

10:48.380 --> 10:49.740
what you enter astrocytes,

10:49.740 --> 10:52.580
but they don't express them with your new vectors?

10:52.580 --> 10:54.740
We haven't looked at the genomic level yet,

10:54.740 --> 10:57.380
and I think we're hoping to go with methods

10:57.380 --> 11:00.580
that were developed such as Spectre or AV Zombie

11:00.580 --> 11:02.220
to look into the genomic level as well

11:02.220 --> 11:04.300
to see where these vectors are actually ending up.

11:04.300 --> 11:06.620
But at the current moment, we don't really know.

11:06.620 --> 11:07.780
Okay, thank you.

11:13.260 --> 11:14.580
Could I ask you a quick question?

11:14.580 --> 11:15.620
Oh, yes, of course.

11:15.620 --> 11:19.020
So if you're talking about modulating

11:19.020 --> 11:22.140
and thinking about the Goldilocks effect,

11:22.140 --> 11:27.140
analytically, how would you come to this Goldilocks

11:27.660 --> 11:29.860
composition, so...

11:29.860 --> 11:30.700
I think that's a really...

11:30.700 --> 11:32.100
And also another thing,

11:32.100 --> 11:35.260
are you looking chanced into one species,

11:35.260 --> 11:36.740
I mean, one strain of mice,

11:36.740 --> 11:39.540
or have you looked into Balbsey as well,

11:39.540 --> 11:44.540
because we have a lot of precedents regarding C57 BL6,

11:44.580 --> 11:47.740
you know, having a very different tropism

11:47.740 --> 11:50.500
versus Balbsey, we're being very different.

11:50.500 --> 11:52.620
Definitely, so regarding your first point

11:52.620 --> 11:55.300
about how are we defining the Goldilocks zone,

11:55.300 --> 11:56.500
we're actually hoping to take more

11:56.540 --> 12:00.620
of a molecular approach by determining the KD values

12:00.620 --> 12:04.580
of their binding interactions using species such as BLI.

12:05.660 --> 12:08.580
And by doing so, we can start to have this defined region

12:08.580 --> 12:13.220
of the preferred optimal rate of binding of virtual cytosis.

12:13.220 --> 12:15.220
As for the second question, I think it's really important

12:15.220 --> 12:17.860
because I work in non-traditional organisms as well.

12:18.940 --> 12:23.140
Full disclosure, these animals are C57s that we tested them in.

12:23.140 --> 12:26.020
But interestingly, LRP6, if you guys don't know,

12:26.020 --> 12:29.300
is a highly conserved receptor with over 98%

12:29.300 --> 12:31.860
sequence similarity between mice and humans.

12:31.860 --> 12:35.500
And so, hopefully, the same patterns do hold true

12:35.500 --> 12:36.900
in other strains of mice as well,

12:36.900 --> 12:38.380
but we definitely do have to test them

12:38.380 --> 12:40.460
and be able to see if this is true.

12:40.460 --> 12:43.580
Yeah, and perhaps you're going up to NHBs as well,

12:43.580 --> 12:46.980
the next two. That'll be really exciting if we can.

12:46.980 --> 12:48.500
All right, thank you very much.

12:48.500 --> 12:49.340
Thank you so much.

12:51.340 --> 12:52.180
Thank you.

12:52.180 --> 12:53.020
Thank you.

12:53.020 --> 12:53.860
Thank you.

12:53.860 --> 12:54.700
Thank you.

12:54.700 --> 12:55.540
Thank you.

12:55.540 --> 12:56.380
So I'm...

